# Rigor core overlay — supplemental core signatures, rbs 3.x line only.
#
# --- Hash#transform_keys / #transform_keys! with a replacements hash ---
#
# Ruby 3.0 added the replacements-hash argument
# (`h.transform_keys({ a: :z }) { |k| k.to_s }`), and rbs 4.0 declares it.
# Every rbs 3.x release declares only the blockless `Enumerator` form and
# the block form, both with no positional parameter:
#
#   def transform_keys: () -> Enumerator[K, Hash[untyped, V]]
#                     | [A] () { (K) -> A } -> Hash[A, V]
#   def transform_keys!: () -> Enumerator[K, self]
#                      | () { (K) -> K } -> self
#
# so under rbs 3.x a correct replacements call reports `call.wrong-arity`
# ("given 1, expected 0"), and `transform_keys!` answers `Enumerator`
# instead of the receiver.
#
# The overloads below are rbs 4.2's, prepended to 3.x's own through
# `| ...`. `RbsLoader::RBS_LINE_CORE_OVERLAYS` loads this file only on
# the rbs 3.x line: on 4.x the continuation would add a second copy of
# the overloads upstream already declares.
#
# Two spellings differ from rbs 4.2 so that every name resolves on every
# rbs 3.x release the gemspec admits:
#
# * `hash[K, V]` is written out as `Hash[K, V] | _ToHash[K, V]`: the
#   `hash` alias arrived in rbs 3.3, `_ToHash` is in 3.0.
# * `hash[_Key, K2]`'s key is `untyped`: `Hash::_Key` arrived in rbs 3.4.
#   It constrains only `hash` / `eql?`, which every object answers.
#
# The non-bang return type is answered ahead of either declaration by
# `MethodDispatcher::HashTransformKeysFolding`.
class Hash[unchecked out K, unchecked out V]
  def transform_keys: [K2] (Hash[untyped, K2] | _ToHash[untyped, K2] replacements) -> Hash[K | K2, V]
                    | [K2] (Hash[untyped, K2] | _ToHash[untyped, K2] replacements) { (K old_key) -> K2 } -> Hash[K2, V]
                    | ...

  def transform_keys!: (Hash[K, K] | _ToHash[K, K] replacements) ?{ (K key) -> K } -> self
                     | ...
end
